            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>



Overall, your house is already quite airtight for it's age, with 2.65 air changes per hour. During the blower door test, I identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the exterior doors where there may be wear or damage to the weatherstripping



** The top of the concrete wall in your laundry room 



** Around the attic hatch - there is a gasket around part of the hatch, but making sure that this gasket is complete could eliminate some air leakage



** At the joints between the wall and the floor, between walls, and between the walls and the ceiling



** Around the door to the garage closest to the boiler. This door is a hollow wood interior door, and based on the infrared pictures there may not be weather stripping around this door. 



Savings: A 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss is estimated to save $100 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding. 



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



I also recommend that you insulate the wall between the garage and your laundry room. Technically, the garage is considered unheated space, even though your boiler is located in there. 



Savings: By adding a minimum R15 to all of the above ground exterior walls, you could potentially save $650 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 20.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the DHERP. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



If you are planning on completing a siding retrofit prior to March 31st 2022, please contact me for more information on the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls are concrete  with no insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls. I also recommend air sealing and insulating around the header space in between the floor joists.



Savings: By adding R20 insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $1,070 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 33.1 GJ, and qualify for up to a $500 rebate from the GNWT Energy </foundation>
                <windows>



Most of your windows are double pane, wood frame windows, that do not appear to have any low-E coatings. I recommend upgrading all of the older, double pane windows in your house, as well as your patio door. 



The ideal time to replace your windows is when you are doing an exterior siding replacement and upgrading your exterior insulation. 



I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing all the older windows in your home with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $470 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 14.6 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <doors>

One of the interior doors to your garage is an older hollow wood door. As attached garages are techncically part of your conditioned space, I recommend you upgrade this door to an ENERGY STAR?????? rated insulated doors for our climate zone. 



Rebates: By replacing this older door with one that is ENERGY STAR?????? qualified for our climate zone, you could potentially save $75 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 85% efficient oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. Please note that the energy savings in GJ listed above is for an oil boiler. 



As the hot water is heated using the boiler, if you are looking to reduce your greenhouse gas footprint, I suggest setting the hot water to run off the boiler in the winter, and switching to electricity in the summer. By contrast, using the boiler to heat the hot water over the summer would save you approximately $200 per year, even considering short cycling. 



I have also modelled using your current pellet stove to meet 25% of your heating load. At current prices, this would save you approximately $200 per year, and increase your overall energy use by 2.2 GJ per year. 



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with an 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $1,900 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 37.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



By contrast, replacing your current oil boiler with a new 87% oil boiler will save you $210 per year, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 



Regardless of whether you choose to switch to propane or stay on oil, while updating your boiler you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 5 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of10.4 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current propane furnace vents up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.



I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler are satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler is operating.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - three bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seals well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.21 per liter 

Propane - $0.683 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</AdditionalComments>
